[m
[34mNAME[0m[m
     systemctl - Control the systemd system and service manager[m
[m
[34mSYNOPSIS[0m[m
     systemctl [OPTIONS...] COMMAND [UNIT...][m
[m
[34mDESCRIPTION[0m[m
     systemctl  may  be  used  to introspect and control the state of the "systemd" system and service manager. Please refer to [34msystemd[0m[35m([0m[33m1[0m[35m)[0m for an introduction into the basic[m
     concepts and functionality this tool manages.[m
[m
[34mCOMMANDS[0m[m
     The following commands are understood:[m
[m
   Unit Commands (Introspection and Modification)[m
     list-units [PATTERN...][m
         List units that systemd currently has in memory. This includes units that are either referenced directly or through a dependency, units that are pinned by  applica‐[m
         tions programmatically, or units that were active in the past and have failed. By default, only units which are active, have pending jobs, or have failed are shown;[m
         this  can  be changed with option --all. If one or more PATTERNs are specified, only units matching one of them are shown. The units that are shown are additionally[m
         filtered by --type= and --state= if those options are specified.[m
[m
         Note that this command does not show unit templates, but only instances of unit templates. Units templates that are not instantiated are not runnable, and will thus[m
         never show up in the output of this command. Specifically this means that foo@.service will never be shown in this list —— unless instantiated, e.g. as foo@bar.ser‐[m
         vice. Use list-unit-files (see below) for listing installed unit template files.[m
[m
         Produces output similar to[m
[m
               UNIT                         LOAD   ACTIVE SUB     DESCRIPTION[m
               sys-module-fuse.device       loaded active plugged /sys/module/fuse[m
               -.mount                      loaded active mounted Root Mount[m
               boot-efi.mount               loaded active mounted /boot/efi[m
               systemd-journald.service     loaded active running Journal Service[m
               systemd-logind.service       loaded active running Login Service[m
             ● user@1000.service            loaded failed failed  User Manager for UID 1000[m
               ...[m
               systemd-tmpfiles-clean.timer loaded active waiting Daily Cleanup of Temporary Directories[m
[m
